{"causes":["The rise of children's literature as a respected genre during the late 20th century allowed for complex characters like Matilda to emerge.","Social changes in attitudes toward education and child empowerment in the 1980s influenced the themes in Dahl's narrative.","The popularity of Roald Dahl's previous works established a significant audience for Matilda's story, leading to its widespread recognition."],"effects":["Matilda's story has inspired educational reforms, emphasizing the importance of nurturing individual talents in children.","The character has influenced cultural representations of childhood, challenging stereotypes about intelligence and resilience.","The adaptations of Matilda into stage and film formats have broadened its reach, embedding the character in contemporary popular culture."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"roald-dahl","sourceName":"Roald Dahl","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"matilda","targetName":"Matilda","context":"In 1988, Roald Dahl created the character Matilda Wormwood in his novel 'Matilda', which explores themes of intelligence and resilience."},{"sourceSlug":"miss-trunchbull","sourceName":"Miss Trunchbull","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"matilda","targetName":"Matilda","context":"Miss Trunchbull, as Matilda's antagonist, defines the obstacles that Matilda must overcome, highlighting the theme of resistance against tyranny."},{"sourceSlug":"england","sourceName":"England","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"matilda","targetName":"Matilda","context":"The story of Matilda takes place in England, reflecting British culture and societal norms of the late 20th century."},{"sourceSlug":"matilda","sourceName":"Matilda","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"education-reform","targetName":"Education Reform","context":"Matilda's narrative has influenced discussions on education reform, advocating for the recognition of children's unique abilities."},{"sourceSlug":"musical-adaptation","sourceName":"Musical Adaptation of Matilda","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"cultural-icon","targetName":"Cultural Icon","context":"The musical adaptation of Matilda has created a cultural icon that represents childhood empowerment, inspiring children and adults alike."}],"places":[{"name":"England","role":"Setting"},{"name":"London, England","role":"Cultural Impact"},{"name":"West End, England","role":"Theater Adaptation"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Matilda","type":"Reference article","slug":"matilda"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1426188","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilda_(novel)"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Matilda contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Matilda is a children's novel by the British author Roald Dahl (1916–1990), illustrated by Quentin Blake, first published by Jonathan Cape in October 1988 — Dahl's penultimate children's novel and one of the best-selling children's books of the late 20th century, having sold over 17 million copies worldwide. The novel narrates the story of Matilda Wormwood, a precociously intelligent and widely read five-year-old girl who is dismissed and neglected by her shallow, television-addicted parents and the bullying headmistress Miss Trunchbull, but is recognised and loved by her teacher Miss Honey — and who develops telekinetic powers that she uses to overthrow the tyrannical Miss Trunchbull and secure a happy future for herself and Miss Honey.

Matilda is a classic example of Dahl's characteristic literary formula: the intelligent, sensitive child protagonist who suffers under the cruelty of brutal adults, develops special powers, and achieves a liberating and often violent revenge against those who oppressed them (Charlie and the Chocolate Factory, James and the Giant Peach). The novel's celebration of reading and intellectual curiosity — Matilda reads voraciously from the public library, consuming Dickens, Hemingway, Steinbeck, and Kipling — made it a book about books, unusual in children's literature, and contributed to its popularity among bookish children and their parents.

Matilda has had an exceptional afterlife in popular culture — it was adapted as a feature film by Danny DeVito (1996) and as a West End and Broadway musical (Matilda the Musical, Royal Shakespeare Company, 2011) — the RSC's Matilda the Musical won seven Olivier Awards and four Tony Awards, and its global tour has played to millions of audiences, making it one of the most successful theatrical adaptations of a children's book in history. Dahl's novel has been particularly influential in children's literature for its celebration of reading as a refuge and liberation, and its unflinching portrait of adult cruelty toward children.","subjects":["British Literature","20th Century","Roald Dahl","Children's Literature","Reading Advocacy","Musical Theatre","Child Protagonist","Fantasy Fiction"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Matilda's character has shaped modern children's literature, influencing educational practices and cultural perceptions of childhood; her story has sold millions of copies and inspired adaptations, emphasizing the importance of nurturing intelligence in young people.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
